> Here's something else I do very consistently, which may be a game 
> exploit but I do it anyway:

Rule of thumb: if the game allows you to do it using only in-game
methods, it's not an exploit.  What you describe below is not an
exploit, for example; it's simply using the features provided.

Building 14+ GB of hash tables to predict the state of the RNG on DL1 so you
can know when a fountain quaff will provide a wish?  _that's_ an exploit.

> For each un-ID'd potion, I stash everything that can be "applied" and 
> a-pply.  If nethack would let me do it, I know I have potion of oil.
>
> I think this will also fall under your category of "too anal-retentive 
> careful to consider" but I do it.

Yes.  Just a)pply all your potions if you're looking to identify oil.
Then, when one lights up, snuff it out.  Presto -- you've found oil.

This is an excellent example.  You're going about things the hard way;
you should really bother to learn a bit more about the _game itself_,
rather than construct elaborate plans around incorrect assumptions (in
this case, that a potion of oil would be used up if a)pplied).

